Part 2 - Identifying the Data

1. What data is needed?
I would need data from past credit card transactions. This includes the transaction amount, when the transaction occurred, information about
the transaction, and whether it was fraudulent or legitimate. The AI would need examples of both normal and fraudulent transactions so it 
can learn the differences between them.

2. Where could the data come from?
The data could come from banks, credit card companies, and payment processing systems that keep records of customer transactions. For this
project, I would use the Credit Card Fraud Detection dataset from Kaggle (https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mlg-ulb/creditcardfraud), 
which contains real credit card transaction data.

3. How much historical data might be useful?
A large amount of historical transaction data would be useful because fraud happens much less often than normal transactions. The Kaggle
dataset contains 284,807 transactions, with only 492 identified as fraud. Having many transactions gives the AI more examples to learn from
and helps it recognize unusual patterns.

4. What would be the most important variables?
Some of the most important variables would be the transaction amount, time of the transaction, patterns within the transaction data, and
whether the transaction was actually fraudulent. The Kaggle dataset includes: Time, Amount, V1-V28, and Class. V1-V28 are anonymized
features, and Class dentifies a transaction as fraudulent or legitimate.

5. What could be missing or inaccurate?
Some useful information may be missing because the Kaggle dataset protects customers' privacy. For example, it does not provide clear
details such as the customer's identity, merchant, purchase location, or type of purchase. There could also be transactions labeled as
legitimate that were actually fraudulent but were never reported or detected.
